issingness indicators) -> LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000, random_state=42). Report validation accuracy against majority-class rate (~0.62) and a Sex-only rule (~0.74–0.78). Hypothesis: baseline lands near 0.78–0.82 and beats both floors. Runtime seconds. This is the reference all later rounds compare to.\n- ROUND 2 — Feature engineering from Name/Ticket/Cabin/Family. Hypothesis: Title (Mr/Mrs/Miss/Master/Rare via regex r\',\\s*([A-Za-z]+)\\.\'), family size (SibSp+Parch+1), is_alone, Cabin deck letter, Ticket prefix, and Fare-per-person lift accuracy beyond Round 1. Motivate by known Titanic signal: women+children in higher classes survive more, large families less, Title proxies missing Age. Plan: same fixed split, same LogisticRegression family, then a shallow DecisionTree depth≤4 to probe non-linearity. Impute missing Age using per-Title median computed on the 712 training rows only. Accept only if fixed validation accuracy improves. Expected +1 to +3 pp. Fit all imputers/encoders on training rows only. Budget: a few seconds.\n- ROUND 3 — Model comparison under the fixed split. Hypothesis: tree ensembles (HistGradientBoostingClassifier(max_iter=300), RandomForest n_estimators=400 with coarse max_depth∈{4,6,8}, min_samples_leaf∈{1,3,5}, ≤12 combos per family) outperform the linear model on the engineered feature set. Share one ColumnTransformer across candidates so preprocessing is identical. For stability also run 5-fold StratifiedKFold inside the 712 training rows, but the fixed 179-row validation is decisive. Also test a soft-vote ensemble of the best two. Persist a comparison table (model, features, val acc) as an artifact; serialize only the winner. n_jobs=4, seed 42, coarse search only. Runtime <2 min CPU.\n- ROUND 4 — Error analysis, calibration check, interpretability and final artifact. Hypothesis: residual errors cluster in ambiguous buckets — 1st/2nd-class males, 3rd-class boys, missing-Age adults, rare titles. Bucket validation errors by (Sex, Pclass