lue open experiment from round-001). Hypothesis: the fixed 0.5 blend weight and max_depth=6/min_samples_leaf=4 were not optimized; a small coarse sweep (blend weight ∈ {0.4,0.5,0.6,0.7}; ExtraTrees max_depth ∈ {5,6,7,8}, min_samples_leaf ∈ {2,3,4,6}) selected by internal 5-fold CV on the 712 train rows may improve the fixed validation beyond noise. Procedure: select on CV only, then evaluate the single CV-chosen setting once on the fixed 179-row validation; accept only if it beats 0.8492. n_jobs=4, seed 42, ≤16 combos per family, runtime <2 min. Persist comparison table.\n- ROUND 5 — Add a calibrated HistGradientBoosting as a third voter (hypothesis test). Hypothesis: standalone RF/GB regressed in round-001, but a small HistGradientBoostingClassifier (max_iter≈200–300, learning_rate≈0.05, max_depth small) as a third soft-voter with weight chosen by internal CV may add diversity and beat the two-model blend on fixed validation. Constraint: only replace the incumbent if fixed validation > 0.8492; keep the weight chosen on CV, not the validation set. Log as comparison table row; if it regresses, keep the two-model blend and record the negative result.\n- ROUND 6 — Probability calibration and threshold check (diagnostic first). Hypothesis: threshold 0.5 may not be optimal for accuracy, and calibration may change the picture. Plan: on internal 5-fold CV (712 train rows) fit Platt/isotonic calibration on the incumbent blend; produce a CV-based threshold sweep. Keep threshold 0.5 for the reported metric unless a CV-selected threshold beats the 0.8492 fixed-validation score; document the sweep as diagnostic artifact only, do not tune on the fixed validation.\n- ROUND 7 — Error analysis and final artifact. Hypothesis: residual errors cluster in ambiguous buckets (1st/2nd-class males, 3rd-class boys, missing-Age adults, rare titles). Diagnostic: bucket validation errors by (Sex, Pclass, Age-isna) and print TP/FP/TN/FN counts; report permutation importance / LR coefficients to confirm sensible signal use. Only if a bucket is systematically wrong, test one targeted interaction (Sex×Pclass or Title×Pclass) and accept only if fixed validation improves >0.5 pp beyond the CV-selected incumbent. Final deliverable: serialize the single winning Pipeline/blend (preprocess+model) via joblib; write work/val_predictions.csv (179 rows, PassengerId+Survived, aligned to validation.csv order), predictions.csv (418 rows, header, exactly PassengerId,Survived, int 0/1, test order preserved, no index), work/metrics.json (fixed-validation accuracy + CV diagnostics), and work/README.txt inference note stating proba≥0.5→1 and that validation and test predictions come from the same winning model.</ideas>\n<local_experiments>\n<experiment>\n<id>round-001-experiment-01</id>\n<title>Titanic Logistic Baseline</title>\n<description>Hypothesis: Titanic survival is largely driven by sex, class, age and family structure; a regularized linear model on well-engineered features (title extracted from Name, family size, alone flag) with median-imputed Age/Fare plus missing indicators would outperform a raw-feature baseline. A later soft-voting ensemble with ExtraTrees was tested and scored the highest.\n\nData processing:\n- Read local train_data/train.csv (712 labeled rows), validation_data/validation.csv (179 feature-only rows), predict_data/test.csv (418 rows). No external data, no gender_submission.csv.\n- Note: the fixed 179-row validation set is supplied pre-split (validation.csv); the earlier re-split of the 712-row train set produced 143 rows and failed evaluation. Corrected to train on all 712 rows and predict the supplied 179-row validation.csv by PassengerId.\n- Feature engineering (deterministic, fit-safe): FamilySize=SibSp+Parch+1, IsAlone=(FamilySize==1), Title from Name regex ", ([^.]+)." with Mlle/Ms→Miss, Mme→Mrs, rare→"Rare".\n- Preprocessing in a ColumnTransformer fit on training rows only: StandardScaler on numeric (Pclass,SibSp,Parch,FamilySize,IsAlone)