[2026-10-18 23:31:06,157 LLM (Agent) INFO] [{'role': 'system', 'content': '\nYou are refining BizMind ML\'s local experiment backlog.\n\n<task_desc>background: 使用 Titanic 乘客信息构造本地机器学习自迭代测试。\nobjective: 根据乘客特征预测 Survived，0 表示未生存，1 表示生存。\ndescription: \'表格二分类任务。train.csv 有 891 行已标注数据，test.csv 有 418 行无标签数据。\n\n  先训练简单 CPU 基线，再根据固定本地验证集的 Accuracy 改进特征、模型或参数。\n\n  使用提供的数据独立完成实验，不查找外部标签、历史解答或排行榜成绩。\n\n  \'\nevaluation:\n  primary_metric: accuracy\n  direction: maximize\n  formula: 正确预测数 / 验证样本总数；取值 0 到 1。\n  validation: \'使用 sklearn.model_selection.train_test_split(test_size=0.2, random_state=42,\n\n    stratify=train[\'\'Survived\'\'])，保留原始行顺序作为划分输入，得到 712 行训练、179 行验证。\n\n    该划分只生成一次，所有自迭代轮次使用同一组 PassengerId。\n\n    验证特征移除 Survived，标签保存在评估器私有目录；编码代理不得读取验证标签。\n\n    预测必须根据 PassengerId 与验证标签逐行对齐；拒绝缺失、重复、额外 ID 或非 0/1 值。\n\n    \'\n  test_scoring: test.csv 没有真实标签，只生成预测，不能报告官方测试集准确率。\nconstraints:\n- 只读取运行时映射的本地 train_data/train.csv 和 predict_data/test.csv，不下载数据或模型。\n- 不使用 gender_submission.csv 训练或评分，它只是提交格式示例，不是真实标签。\n- 预处理、缺失值填充、编码、特征选择只在训练子集拟合，验证和预测只做 transform。\n- 优先使用 sklearn 或 LightGBM 的 CPU 模型，不依赖 CUDA、MPS 或大型预训练模型。\n- 固定随机种子为 42；限制并行度不超过执行配置，避免大规模网格搜索。\n- 如使用 predict_proba，统一以概率 >= 0.5 转换为 1，否则为 0，再计算准确率和导出预测。\n- 保存与当前验证成绩对应的模型和预处理流程，保持验证与测试预测来自同一模型。\noutput:\n  prediction_file: predictions.csv\n  columns:\n  - PassengerId\n  - Survived\n  format: \'预测文件必须含表头、418 行、恰好两列 PassengerId 和 Survived，不输出额外索引。\n\n    PassengerId 保持 test.csv 原始顺序，Survived 为整数 0 或 1，不是概率。\n\n    另存验证预测 CSV，使用相同两列并与固定验证集对齐，交由独立评估器计算指标。\n\n    \'\n  artifacts: 保存模型、预处理流程、验证指标和推理说明，并按编码代理协议返回验证及测试预测路径。\ndata:\n  target_column: Survived\n  id_column: PassengerId\n  schema:\n    PassengerId: 乘客唯一标识；用于预测对齐，不作为基线模型特征。\n    Survived: 目标标签；0 未生存，1 生存，仅训练集包含此列。\n    Pclass: 船票等级；1 一等，2 二等，3 三等。\n    Name: 乘客姓名。\n    Sex: 性别。\n    Age: 年龄，单位为年；可能缺失，婴儿年龄可为小数。\n    SibSp: 同船兄弟姐妹及配偶人数。\n    Parch: 同船父母及子女人数。\n    Ticket: 船票编号。\n    Fare: 票价；可能缺失。\n    Cabin: 舱位编号；可能缺失。\n    Embarked: 登船港口；C 为 Cherbourg，Q 为 Queenstown，S 为 Southampton，可能缺失。\n\n\n## Runtime dataset mapping (relative to the active task input directory)\n- Labeled training data: train_data/train.csv\n- Unlabeled prediction data: predict_data/test.csv\nThese paths override source paths or example filenames in the business description.\nIn a coding notebook, read from ../input/data/ using these relative\npaths. This points to the evaluator\'s public split, not the\noriginal training source. Preserve prediction row identifiers and output format.\nDo not modify source files; extract archives and write generated files in the\nworking/public directories. Never read or copy hidden validation labels.\n</task_desc>\n<execution_environment>Local execution platform: Darwin; CPU logical cores: 8.\nSelected training device: cpu.\nUse CPU training. Do not require CUDA, NVIDIA tools, or an MPS accelerator.\nIn notebook code, os.environ[\'BIZMIND_DEVICE\'] identifies the selected backend. Respect CPU limits and scale batch/model sizes to available memory; no GPU memory reservation is provided.\nCPU core limit per session: 4.\nMaximum time per code cell: 3600 seconds.\n</execution_environment>\n<ideas>- ROUND 1 — Fix the evaluation protocol (root cause of the earlier failure) + cheap baseline. Lesson from round-001: the 179-row validation set is supplied pre-split as validation_data/validation.csv (feature-only); do NOT re-split the 712-row train.csv — the earlier re-split produced 143 rows and failed evaluation. Protocol: read train_data/train.csv (712 labeled rows), validation_data/validation.csv (179 feature-only rows), predict_data/test.csv (418 rows). Persist ordered validation PassengerId list to work/val_ids.json once and assert every later round reuses identical IDs. Data-quality audit: assert no duplicate PassengerId across train/val/test; Survived∈{0,1}; print NaN fractions and value counts per column; check unseen Embarked/Cabin categories and Age/Fare ranges in test. Baseline Pipeline (fit on all 712 training rows only): ColumnTransformer = Sex one-hot + Pclass/SibSp/Parch numeric + Age/Fare median impute with missingness indicators → LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000, random_state=42). Report validation accuracy vs majority floor (~0.615) and Sex-only rule (~0.776). Hypothesis: baseline lands ~0.81–0.82. This is the reference all later rounds compare to.\n- ROUND 2 — Confirmed feature set as the working baseline (already measured, keep as the standard). Evidence from round-001: raw LR ~0.816 → +Title/FamilySize/IsAlone ~0.8436 (best single model, L1-tuned LR). Feature engineering to keep identical across all rounds: FamilySize=SibSp+Parch+1, IsAlone=(FamilySize==1), Title from Name regex ", ([^.]+)." with Mlle/Ms→Miss, Mme→Mrs, rare→"Rare". Preprocessing in one shared ColumnTransformer fit on training rows only: StandardScaler on [Pclass,SibSp,Parch,FamilySize,IsAlone]; SimpleImputer(median, add_indicator=True)+StandardScaler on [Age,Fare]; SimpleImputer(constant \'M\')+OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\'ignore\') on [Sex,Embarked,Title]. Do not re-test variants already shown to regress (Deck, LogFare, AgeBin, class_weight, RF standalone) unless part of a new hypothesis; they added no gain in round-001.\n- ROUND 3 — Confirmed best: LR+ExtraTrees soft-vote (keep as incumbent, guard against noise). Evidence: 0.5*LogisticRegression(max_iter=2000, random_state=42) + 0.5*ExtraTreesClassifier(n_estimators=400, max_depth=6, min_samples_leaf=4, random_state=42, n_jobs=4), proba≥0.5→1, scored 0.8492 vs 0.8436 single LR. Caveat from round-001: +0.56 pp on 179 rows is within noise (±1–2 pts). Treat 0.8492 as incumbent but do not treat it as decisively better than 0.8436. Any new candidate must beat 0.8492 on the fixed 179-row validation to replace the incumbent; also report an internal 5-fold StratifiedKFold accuracy on the 712 train rows as a stability diagnostic (do not tune on the fixed validation).\n- ROUND 4 — Re-tune blend weight and ExtraTrees leaf/depth via internal CV (highest-value open experiment from round-001). Hypothesis: the fixed 0.5 blend weight and max_depth=6/min_samples_leaf=4 were not optimized; a small coarse sweep (blend weight ∈ {0.4,0.5,0.6,0.7}; ExtraTrees max_depth ∈ {5,6,7,8}, min_samples_leaf ∈ {2,3,4,6}) selected by internal 5-fold CV on the 712 train rows may improve the fixed validation beyond noise. Procedure: select on CV only, then evaluate the single CV-chosen setting once on the fixed 179-row validation; accept only if it beats 0.8492. n_jobs=4, seed 42, ≤16 combos per family, runtime <2 min. Persist comparison table.\n- ROUND 5 — Add a calibrated HistGradientBoosting as a third voter (hypothesis test). Hypothesis: standalone RF/GB regressed in round-001, but a small HistGradientBoostingClassifier (max_iter≈200–300, learning_rate≈0.05, max_depth small) as a third soft-voter with weight chosen by internal CV may add diversity and beat the two-model blend on fixed validation. Constraint: only replace the incumbent if fixed validation > 0.8492; keep the weight chosen on CV, not the validation set. Log as comparison table row; if it regresses, keep the two-model blend and record the negative result.\n- ROUND 6 — Probability calibration and threshold check (diagnostic first). Hypothesis: threshold 0.5 may not be optimal for accuracy, and calibration may change the picture. Plan: on internal 5-fold CV (712 train rows) fit Platt/isotonic calibration on the incumbent blend; produce a CV-based threshold sweep. Keep threshold 0.5 for the reported metric unless a CV-selected threshold beats the 0.8492 fixed-validation score; document the sweep as diagnostic artifact only, do not tune on the fixed validation.\n- ROUND 7 — Error analysis and final artifact. Hypothesis: residual errors cluster in ambiguous buckets (1st/2nd-class males, 3rd-class boys, missing-Age adults, rare titles). Diagnostic: bucket validation errors by (Sex, Pclass, Age-isna) and print TP/FP/TN/FN counts; report permutation importance / LR coefficients to confirm sensible signal use. Only if a bucket is systematically wrong, test one targeted interaction (Sex×Pclass or Title×Pclass) and accept only if fixed validation improves >0.5 pp beyond the CV-selected incumbent. Final deliverable: serialize the single winning Pipeline/blend (preprocess+model) via joblib; write work/val_predictions.csv (179 rows, PassengerId+Survived, aligned to validation.csv order), predictions.csv (418 rows, header, exactly PassengerId,Survived, int 0/1, test order preserved, no index), work/metrics.json (fixed-validation accuracy + CV diagnostics), and work/README.txt inference note stating proba≥0.5→1 and that validation and test predictions come from the same winning model.\n- ROUND 1 — Protocol fix + data-quality audit + cheap baseline. First re-establish the correct evaluation protocol, since prior rounds failed when re-splitting train.csv (143 rows) instead of using the supplied pre-split holdout. Read train_data/train.csv (712 labeled rows), validation_data/validation.csv (179 feature-only rows), predict_data/test.csv (418 rows). Persist the ordered validation PassengerId list to work/val_ids.json once and assert all later rounds reuse identical IDs. Audit data quality: no duplicate PassengerId across splits; Survived∈{0,1}; NaN fractions and value counts per column; unseen categories in Embarked/Cabin and Age/Fare ranges in test. Then fit on all 712 rows only a baseline Pipeline: ColumnTransformer = Sex one-hot + Pclass/SibSp/Parch numeric + Age/Fare median-impute with missingness indicators → LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000, random_state=42). Hypothesis: baseline lands ~0.81–0.82, above majority floor (~0.615) and Sex-only rule (~0.776). This is the reference for later rounds. Runtime <1 min, memory trivial.\n- Read the three CSVs from their runtime paths; load data/validation.csv as the authoritative holdout and dump sorted-original-order PassengerId list to work/val_ids.json.\n- Run the data-quality audit script: dedupe checks, label domain check, NaN fractions, value counts, unseen-category and range checks; print a compact report to work/audit.txt.\n- Fit the baseline LogisticRegression Pipeline on all 712 rows and score once on the 179-row holdout; save work/model_baseline.joblib and record accuracy in work/metrics.json.\n- ROUND 2 — Confirmed feature-set baseline (keep as the working standard). Based on prior measured evidence: raw LR ~0.816 → +Title/FamilySize/IsAlone ~0.8436 (best single model, L1-tuned LR). Lock the feature set identical across rounds: FamilySize=SibSp+Parch+1, IsAlone=(FamilySize==1), Title from Name regex ", ([^.]+)." with Mlle/Ms→Miss, Mme→Mrs, rare→"Rare". Shared ColumnTransformer fit on training rows only: StandardScaler on [Pclass,SibSp,Parch,FamilySize,IsAlone]; SimpleImputer(median, add_indicator=True)+StandardScaler on [Age,Fare]; SimpleImputer(constant \'M\')+OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\'ignore\') on [Sex,Embarked,Title]. Validation plan: same fixed 179-row holdout; expected evidence is validation accuracy ≈0.84, materially above the round-1 baseline. Do not re-test variants already shown to regress (Deck, LogFare, AgeBin, class_weight, RF standalone). Runtime <1 min.\n- Implement a single featurize() function used for train/val/test; compute rare-title mapping and any statistics from training rows only.\n- Build the shared ColumnTransformer and wrap it in a Pipeline with LogisticRegression(max_iter=2000, random_state=42) (and optionally an L1-tuned C via internal CV on the 712 rows).\n- Score once on the fixed holdout, write work/model_lr_features.joblib, val/test predictions, and update work/metrics.json.\n- ROUND 3 — Confirmed best incumbent: LR+ExtraTrees soft-vote, guarded against noise. Measured evidence: 0.5*LogisticRegression(max_iter=2000, random_state=42) + 0.5*ExtraTreesClassifier(n_estimators=400, max_depth=6, min_samples_leaf=4, random_state=42, n_jobs=4), proba≥0.5→1, scored 0.8492 vs 0.8436 single LR. Caveat: +0.56 pp on 179 rows is within noise (±1–2 pts), so treat 0.8492 as incumbent but not decisively better. Any new candidate must beat 0.8492 on the fixed holdout to replace it. Also report an internal 5-fold StratifiedKFold accuracy on the 712 train rows purely as a stability diagnostic — never tune on the fixed holdout. Validation plan: reproduce incumbent byte-identically (pin md5 of val/test predictions) so later perturbations are comparable. Runtime <2 min, memory low, n_jobs=4, seed 42.\n- Rebuild the two pipelines with shared preprocessing, fit on the 712 rows, blend probabilities at w=0.5, and threshold at 0.5.\n- Write work/model_blend_best.joblib (dict: lr, et, w, threshold), val/test predictions, and pin the md5s in work/metrics.json.\n- ROUND 4 — Test genuinely new information (highest-value open hypothesis, not more hyperparameter search). Prior evidence shows seven perturbations (blend-weight CV tuning, interaction features, 3-way GB vote, more trees, threshold shift, seed-bagging, LR C) all regressed below 0.8492, and criterion changes were prediction-identical. The strongest untested predictor is Cabin deck (first letter), currently dropped, plus potentially Ticket prefix/family-group survival signal. Validation plan: single controlled addition over the incumbent — add Deck (missing→\'U\') as one-hot via the shared ColumnTransformer, fit-safe (categories only from training), then evaluate once on the fixed holdout. Accept only if it beats 0.8492 (>0.5 pp to exceed noise). Similarly, test one ticket-group feature (group size / survival-independent prefix) in isolation. Do NOT re-run already-rejected perturbations. Report a comparison table; if all regress, keep the incumbent and record negative results. Runtime <3 min, memory low.\n- Extract Deck = Cabin first letter, missing→\'U\'; also parse Ticket prefix (non-numeric leading token, else \'NONE\'); compute group sizes within train only.\n- Add Deck (and separately ticket-group size) to the shared ColumnTransformer; refit LR+ET on 712 rows and score once on the fixed holdout.\n- Log each controlled addition as a row in work/comparison.csv; keep the incumbent if none beats 0.8492, and record the negative evidence.\n- ROUND 5 — Error analysis, calibration diagnostic and final artifact. With the winning model fixed, analyze residual validation errors bucketed by (Sex, Pclass, Age-isna) and rare titles; print TP/FP/TN/FN counts; report LR coefficients / permutation importance to confirm sensible signal use. Run a diagnostic calibration check (Platt/isotonic on internal 5-fold CV of the 712 rows) and a CV-based threshold sweep — keep threshold 0.5 for the reported metric unless a CV-selected threshold actually beats the holdout score; document the sweep as a diagnostic artifact only (never tune on the fixed holdout). Only if a bucket is systematically wrong, test ONE targeted interaction (Sex×Pclass or Title×Pclass) and accept only if the fixed holdout improves >0.5 pp. Final deliverable: serialize the single winning Pipeline/blend via joblib; write work/val_predictions.csv (179 rows aligned to validation order), predictions.csv (418 rows, header, exactly PassengerId,Survived, int 0/1, test order, no index), work/metrics.json (holdout accuracy + CV diagnostics + comparison ledger), and work/README.txt stating proba≥0.5→1 and that val/test predictions come from the same winning model. Runtime <3 min.\n- Build an error-analysis table bucketing holdout errors by Sex, Pclass, Age-missing, Title; print confusion counts and per-bucket error rate.\n- Run the calibration + CV threshold sweep on 712 rows as a diagnostic; keep 0.5 unless a CV-selected threshold beats the holdout (guard against overfitting-on-179).\n- Serialize the winning model with joblib and emit the final predictions.csv, work/val_predictions.csv, work/metrics.json, work/README.txt with strict format/alignment assertions.</ideas>\n<local_experiments>\n<experiment>\n<id>round-001-experiment-01</id>\n<title>Titanic Logistic Baseline</title>\n<description>Hypothesis: Titanic survival is largely driven by sex, class, age and family structure; a regularized linear model on well-engineered features (title extracted from Name, family size, alone flag) with median-imputed Age/Fare plus missing indicators would outperform a raw-feature baseline. A later soft-voting ensemble with ExtraTrees was tested and scored the highest.\n\nData processing:\n- Read local train_data/train.csv (712 labeled rows), validation_data/validation.csv (179 feature-only rows), predict_data/test.csv (418 rows). No external data, no gender_submission.csv.\n- Note: the fixed 179-row validation set is supplied pre-split (validation.csv); the earlier re-split of the 712-row train set produced 143 rows and failed evaluation. Corrected to train on all 712 rows and predict the supplied 179-row validation.csv by PassengerId.\n- Feature engineering (deterministic, fit-safe): FamilySize=SibSp+Parch+1, IsAlone=(FamilySize==1), Title from Name regex ", ([^.]+)." with Mlle/Ms→Miss, Mme→Mrs, rare→"Rare".\n- Preprocessing in a ColumnTransformer fit on training rows only: StandardScaler on numeric (Pclass,SibSp,Parch,FamilySize,IsAlone); Age/Fare median imputation with missing indicator then scaling; Sex/Embarked (missing→\'M\')/Title one-hot with handle_unknown=\'ignore\'.\n\nBest measured model:\n- 0.5 * LogisticRegression(max_iter=2000, random_state=42) + 0.5 * ExtraTreesClassifier(n_estimators=400, max_depth=6, min_samples_leaf=4, random_state=42, n_jobs=4). Soft-vote of predict_proba, threshold ≥0.5 → 1. Seed 42, CPU.\n- This ensemble scored the engine-recorded best validation metric 0.8492, exceeding the single L1-regularized LR (C tuned, 0.8436) and the earlier RF (0.83), LogFare/wt/Deck/AgeBin variants (≤0.8436).\n\nValidation evidence (executed): The evaluator measured the fixed-split accuracy; the best reported is 0.8492 for the LR+ExtraTrees soft-vote. Single-factor results measured along the way: majority floor ~0.615, sex-only ~0.776, baseline LR ~0.816, +Title/FamilySize/IsAlone ~0.8436, RF ~0.832, LogFare ~0.832, class_weight ~0.827.\n\nChanges from parent: started from an LR baseline using raw Pclass/SibSp/Parch/Sex/Embarked with Age/Fare imputation. Added Name-derived Title, FamilySize, IsAlone, standardized numerics, and finally a soft-voting ExtraTrees ensemble.\n\nArtifacts: predictions.csv (418 rows, PassengerId+Survived, test order), work/val_predictions.csv (179 rows aligned to validation.csv), work/model_blend_et.joblib (dict {"lr","et"}), work/metrics.json, work/README.txt. Earlier single-model artifacts retained: work/model_final.joblib.\n\nLoad: joblib.load("work/model_blend_et.joblib"); prob = 0.5*lr.predict_proba(X)[:,1] + 0.5*et.predict_proba(X)[:,1]; Survived = (prob>=0.5).astype(int). Apply the same featurize() to raw rows before predict.</description>\n<code># featurize(df): add FamilySize, IsAlone, Title (Name regex; Mlle/Ms->Miss, Mme->Mrs, rare->Rare)\n# ColumnTransformer:\n#   num = SimpleImputer(median)+StandardScaler on [Pclass,SibSp,Parch,FamilySize,IsAlone]\n#   af  = SimpleImputer(median, add_indicator=True)+StandardScaler on [Age,Fare]\n#   cat = SimpleImputer(constant \'M\')+OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\'ignore\') on [Sex,Embarked,Title]\n# lr = Pipeline(pre, LogisticRegression(max_iter=2000, random_state=42)).fit(X_tr,y_tr)\n# et = Pipeline(pre, ExtraTreesClassifier(n_estimators=400, max_depth=6, min_samples_leaf=4,\n#                                         random_state=42, n_jobs=4)).fit(X_tr,y_tr)\n# prob = 0.5*lr.predict_proba(X)[:,1] + 0.5*et.predict_proba(X)[:,1]\n# Survived = (prob>=0.5).astype(int)\n# val -> work/val_predictions.csv (aligned to validation.csv PassengerId order)\n# test -> predictions.csv (418 rows, header PassengerId,Survived, no index)\n# joblib.dump({"lr":lr,"et":et}, "work/model_blend_et.joblib")</code>\n<validation_metric>Metric↑(0.8492)</validation_metric>\n<suggestions>Observed weaknesses (evidence): The validation set is only 179 rows, so single-trial accuracy swings by ~1–2 points are within noise; the 0.8436→0.8492 gain from the ensemble is plausible but small and could partly be noise. RF, LogFare, class_weight, C-tuning, Deck and AgeBin added no gain; LR on the chosen features is a strong, stable baseline.\n\nLessons (evidence): (1) The validation set is supplied pre-split; do not re-split the 712-row train file (caused the 143-vs-179 failure). (2) Scaling numerics removed lbfgs convergence warnings without changing accuracy. (3) Tree ensembles only helped as a soft complement to LR (LR+ExtraTrees), not standalone (RF/GB regressed).\n\nPrioritized next experiments (hypotheses, not yet run): (1) Re-tune ExtraTrees depth/leaf and the LR/ET blend weight (0.5 fixed here) via internal 5-fold CV on the 712 train rows — test whether the blend weight and leaf size matter beyond noise. (2) Add a small calibrated HistGradientBoosting as a third voter with weight chosen by CV; verify it beats the two-model blend on the fixed 179-row validation. (3) Calibrate probabilities (Platt/isotonic on internal CV) to check if threshold 0.5 remains optimal for accuracy. (4) Confirm run-to-run stability by varying the ET n_jobs/seed-free components; keep seed 42 and report variance across internal CV folds rather than trusting a single fixed-split number.</suggestions>\n</experiment>\n\n\n<experiment>\n<id>round-002-experiment-01</id>\n<title>CV-tuned blend weights & ET leaves</title>\n<description>**Task & metric.** Binary classification of Titanic `Survived` (0/1). Primary metric is accuracy on the fixed 179-row validation holdout; training uses the evaluator-supplied 712 rows of `train_data/train.csv` only, validation/test are transform-only, and predictions must be 0/1 (prob >= 0.5 -> 1).\n\n**Best measured version (canonical incumbent, fixed-validation accuracy 0.8492 — the best score observed across all executed rounds).** A 50/50 soft-vote blend of two CPU models fit on all 712 training rows:\n- LogisticRegression (sklearn default C=1.0, max_iter=2000, random_state=42).\n- ExtraTreesClassifier(n_estimators=400, max_depth=6, min_samples_leaf=4, criterion=\'gini\', random_state=42, n_jobs=4).\n- Blended probability = 0.5*p_LR + 0.5*p_ET; Survived = 1 if prob >= 0.5 else 0.\n\n**Feature processing (shared ColumnTransformer fit on training rows only, embedded in each pipeline):**\n- `FamilySize = SibSp + Parch + 1`, `IsAlone = (FamilySize == 1)`.\n- `Title` extracted from `Name` via regex `,\\s*([^.]+)\\.`; Mlle/Ms -> Miss, Mme -> Mrs; titles with training count < 10 mapped to `Rare`.\n- `Embarked` missing -> \'M\'.\n- Numeric `[Pclass, SibSp, Parch, FamilySize, IsAlone]`: median impute + StandardScaler.\n- `[Age, Fare]`: median impute with add_indicator + StandardScaler.\n- Categorical `[Sex, Embarked, Title]`: constant \'M\' impute + OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\'ignore\').\n\n**Validation evidence (executed, evaluator-scored):**\n- Incumbent achieved 0.8492 on the fixed 179-row holdout (best so far; reproduced byte-identically, val md5 `cceba64816`, test md5 `42c718fef6`).\n- Seven controlled perturbations were each executed and all failed to beat 0.8492: round-4 CV-tuned blend (w=0.4, ET d=6/leaf=3) = 0.8436; interaction features (Sex_Pclass, FamilySize_bin) = 0.8212; LR+ET+GB 3-way vote = 0.8436; ET n_estimators 400->800 = 0.8436; decision threshold 0.45 = 0.8380; 5-seed bagging = 0.8436; CV-selected LR C=0.3 = 0.8380. ET criterion=\'entropy\' produced byte-identical predictions to gini (no change in metric). Each was reverted to the incumbent.\n\n**Changes from the parent.** The parent is the same LR+ExtraTrees soft-vote incumbent at w=0.5 with depth=6, leaf=4 (scored 0.8492). The best version is identical to the parent; all substantive changes attempted this round regressed, so the parent recipe was retained and re-verified.\n\n**Artifacts & loading (in `work/`):**\n- `work/model_blend_best.joblib`: dict with keys `lr`, `et` (full sklearn Pipelines including preprocessing), `w` (0.5), `n_estimators`, `threshold` (0.5), `lr_C` (1.0), `et_criterion` (\'gini\'). Load with `joblib.load("work/model_blend_best.joblib")`; predict via `w*lr.predict_proba(X)[:,1] + (1-w)*et.predict_proba(X)[:,1] >= threshold`, where `X` is the featurized DataFrame (FamilySize/IsAlone/Title/Embarked derived, raw columns selected).\n- `predictions.csv`: 418-row test predictions (PassengerId, Survived), original test order.\n- `work/val_predictions.csv`: 179-row validation predictions aligned to validation order.\n- `work/metrics.json`: config, metric 0.8492, full perturbation ledger, pinned md5s.\n- `work/README.txt`: inference instructions.\n\n**Reproducibility.** Seed 42, CPU, n_jobs=4, no downloads; val/test predictions come from the same single model and were asserted byte-identical to the pinned md5s on reload.</description>\n<code># --- Best measured pipeline (canonical incumbent) ---\nimport joblib, numpy as np, pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, OneHotEncoder\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import ExtraTreesClassifier\n\nSEED = 42\n# 1) load train/val/test (relative to ../input/data/)\n# 2) featurize: FamilySize, IsAlone, Title(rare<10->Rare via training counts), Embarked missing->\'M\'\n#    (same featurize applied to train/val/test; rare mapping computed from training Title counts only)\n# 3) shared ColumnTransformer:\n#      num[Pclass,SibSp,Parch,FamilySize,IsAlone] -> median impute + StandardScaler\n#      af [Age,Fare]                              -> median impute(add_indicator) + StandardScaler\n#      cat[Sex,Embarked,Title]                    -> constant \'M\' impute + OneHotEncoder(ignore)\n#    embedded in each Pipeline so preprocessing is fit inside the model pipeline on training rows only.\nlr = Pipeline([("pre", make_pre()), ("lr", LogisticRegression(max_iter=2000, random_state=SEED))]).fit(X, y)\net = Pipeline([("pre", make_pre()), ("et", ExtraTreesClassifier(\n        n_estimators=400, max_depth=6, min_samples_leaf=4,\n        criterion="gini", random_state=SEED, n_jobs=4))]).fit(X, y)\n# 4) blend: prob = 0.5*lr.proba + 0.5*et.proba ; Survived = (prob >= 0.5)\n# 5) write predictions.csv (418) and work/val_predictions.csv (179) from this single model;\n#    persist joblib.dump({"lr","et","w":0.5,"threshold":0.5,...}, "work/model_blend_best.joblib")\n# 6) assert val/test PassengerId alignment, 0/1 domain, and reload-reproducibility.\n\n# Failed approach summary: 7 perturbations (CV blend tuning, interaction features, 3-way GB vote,\n# more trees, threshold 0.45, seed-bagging, LR C=0.3) each executed and regressed below 0.8492;\n# entropy criterion gave identical predictions. All were reverted to the incumbent.</code>\n<validation_metric>Metric↑(0.8492)</validation_metric>\n<suggestions>**Observed weaknesses (evidence):**\n- The 179-row holdout is tiny; a single row is worth ~0.0056 accuracy, so perturbations move the score by only 1-4 rows and are dominated by noise. Round-4 and round-18 CV gains on 712 rows (+0.003 to +0.007 in 5-fold) failed to transfer to the holdout (both scored ~0.838-0.844) — CV-based selection is not a reliable proxy here.\n- Every added feature/model complexity (interactions, GradientBoosting, seed bagging, stronger ET) regressed; the incumbent is a stable optimum on this specific holdout.\n- Threshold and criterion changes either regressed (0.45) or were prediction-identical (entropy), implying the model\'s decision boundary is already saturated for these features.\n\n**Prioritized next experiments (hypotheses, not yet demonstrated):**\n1. Genuine new signal, not model tuning: the strongest untested Titanic predictor is likely `Cabin` deck (first letter), currently dropped. A fit-safe deck feature (missing -> \'U\') could add real information the current feature set lacks — test as a single controlled addition over the incumbent.\n2. Family/ticket-group survival features: shared `Ticket` prefix and group-size interactions sometimes carry signal; test one at a time and only keep if it beats 0.8492 on the fixed holdout (guard against the observed overfitting-on-179 pattern).\n3. If further tuning is pursued, consider that the evaluator\'s 712/179 split is the only reliable measuring stick — do not trust internal CV for final selection, since two CV-selected configs both regressed.\n4. Do NOT re-run already-rejected perturbations (interactions, GB, more trees, threshold shift, seed-bag, LR C, criterion) — they have executed negative evidence.\n\n**Lesson:** On this holdout the incumbent LR+ET (w=0.5, d=6, leaf=4) is the reproducible best; future rounds should either accept it as final or justify a *new-information* feature (e.g., Cabin deck) with a single controlled test rather than more hyperparameter search.</suggestions>\n</experiment>\n\n<failed_attempts></failed_attempts></local_experiments>\nThe business requirements define the target, primary metric, validation rules,\npermitted inputs and output schema. Use only supplied local data and completed\nlocal experiments. Do not download datasets, pretrained models or predictions.\nStart with a simple reproducible baseline. Keep the evaluator\'s split fixed,\nfit preprocessing on training rows only, and never read hidden validation labels.\nEvery improvement needs a testable hypothesis and comparable validation evidence.\nTreat failed and unscored attempts as lessons, not successful results.\nUse CPU when a library lacks support for the selected accelerator.\n\nMerge duplicate ideas and remove disproven or infeasible changes. Preserve useful\ntechnical details and business requirements. Rank actions by measured weaknesses,\nexpected benefit and cost. Keep at most 20 actionable ideas; preserve a baseline\nuntil a valid local result exists. Respond with repeated <idea>...</idea> tag